  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1216 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\n*** PROLONGED HOT AND DRY CONDITIONS ***\n\nThe upper ridge currently centered over Colorado will strengthen\nand expand southeastward, becoming centered over N TX/OK by mid-\nweek. This evolution will finally tug the weak upper low \nnorthwestward, away from the region, and subsidence associated \nwith height rises aloft will further stymie precip development. \nGuidance has consistently suggested this will be a strong ridge, \nwith 500mb heights in the upper 590s. A prolonged period of hot \nand dry weather remains the expectation, and the temperature \nforecast has not changed meaningfully from the previous forecast \ncycle. \n\nThe hottest conditions are expected to occur off the Caprock\nduring the latter half of the work week and into the weekend, and\nthe NBM depicts a 40-70% chance of exceeding Heat Advisory \ncriteria (105F) each day Wednesday through the weekend. Areas on \nthe Caprock will see highs in the upper 90s and low 100s during \nthis same time frame. Warm low temps will limit the nocturnal \nrelief, with lows late in the week and over the weekend only \nfalling into the mid 70s on the Caprock and upper 70s off the \nCaprock. If realized, lows in the upper 70s would be very close to\nrecord warm lows for this time of year. The multi-day nature of \nthis anomalous heat and the limited nocturnal relief will compound\nthe heat impacts. The NWS HeatRisk product shows steadily \nbuilding impacts into the weekend, with Major (level 3 of 4) to \nExtreme (level 4 of 4) impacts across the entire NWS Lubbock CWA \nby the weekend. These levels suggest the heat will affect anyone \nwithout effective cooling and hydration.\n\nWhile the aforementioned Tropical Depression 2 has formed in the\neastern Gulf and is forecast to track west towards LA/SE TX late \nin the work week, the placement of the upper ridge is such that \nthe associated moisture content is unlikely to reach W TX. \nInstead, our potential wrinkle for late in the week is a \nshortwave progged to round the ridge and move into the central \nPlains. Some deterministic guidance also depicts a front shifting \nsouth into the south-central US by the weekend, although this \nfront and its associated forcing appears better defined east of \nour area. These two features offer at least low chance of \nprecip/cloud cover that could keep temps lower than the very hot\nvalues currently forecast. It's a slim chance, but still \nsomething to keep an eye on in an extended forecast that otherwise\nappears dominated by the upper ridge and the associated intense \nheat.",
